Always follow these rules when writing or editing `.tex` content:

## Document Structure

- All chapters are **numbered** — use `\chapter{Title}` with a `\label{chap:key}` on the next line. Do NOT use `\chapter*`.
- Section hierarchy: `\chapter` → `\section` → `\subsection` → `\subsubsection` → `\paragraph` → `\subparagraph`.
- Appendices go after `\appendix` at the end of the document, before `\end{document}`.

## Quotation Marks

- Always use `\enquote{text}` (from the `csquotes` package) — **never** use `''text''` or `"text"`.

## Lists

- For paragraph-spaced lists use `\begin{itemize}`, `\begin{enumerate}`, `\begin{description}`.
- For compact word/phrase lists use `\begin{compactitem}` and `\begin{compactenum}` (from `paralist`).
- Nested lists are supported in both styles.

## Fonts and Emphasis

- Body emphasis: `\textit{text}` (italics)
- Code/monospace: `\texttt{text}`
- Bold: `\textbf{text}`
- Small caps: `\textsc{text}`
- Sans-serif: `\textsf{text}`
- Do **not** change font sizes manually in body text.

## Mathematics

- Inline math: `$...$`
- Display unnumbered: `\[...\]`
- Numbered equation: `\begin{equation}...\end{equation}`
- Multi-line: `\begin{align}`, `\begin{alignat}`, `\begin{multline}`, `\begin{gather}`
- Conditional: `\begin{cases}` inside an `equation` environment
- Always label equations you reference: `\label{eq:key}`, cite with `\eqref{eq:key}`

## Theorems, Lemmas, Proofs

Define once in the preamble, then use `\begin{theorem}`, `\begin{lemma}`, `\begin{proof}`, `\begin{corollary}`, `\begin{definition}`, `\begin{remark}`.

## Figures

- Wrap in `\begin{figure}...\end{figure}` with `\begin{center}` inside.
- Always include `\caption{}` and `\label{fig:key}`.
- Minimum image resolution: 100 px/cm. Optimum: 250 px/cm.

## Tables

- Use `tabularx` with `\textwidth` for full-width tables.
- Use `\toprule`, `\midrule`, `\bottomrule` (booktabs) — never `\hline`.
- Captions go **above** tables (floatrow is configured for this).
- Always include `\caption{}` and `\label{tab:key}`.

## Cross-References

- Label everything: `\label{chap:key}`, `\label{sec:key}`, `\label{fig:key}`, `\label{tab:key}`, `\label{eq:key}`.
- Reference with `Chapter~\ref{chap:key}`, `Figure~\ref{fig:key}`, `Table~\ref{tab:key}`.
- Use `~` before `\ref{}` to prevent line breaks.
- Use `\eqref{eq:key}` for equation references.

## Bibliography and Citations

The bibliography file is [example.bib](mdc:example.bib). The project uses **biblatex with the biber backend and numeric style** (`\usepackage[backend=biber, style=numeric]{biblatex}`).

### Citation commands

- Parenthetical citation (most common): `\parencite{key}` → [1]
- Inline author citation: `\textcite{key}` → Author [1]
- Multiple sources at once: `\parencite{key1,key2}`
- Title only: `\citetitle{key}`
- Author only: `\citeauthor{key}`
- Year only: `\citeyear{key}`
- Footnote with full details: `\footfullcite{key}`
- Print bibliography at end: `\printbibliography[heading=bibintoc]`

### What MUST be cited

Every factual claim that is not your own original work needs a citation. This includes:
- Definitions and explanations of technologies (LLMs, Kubernetes, MCP, RHOAI, etc.)
- Statistics or benchmark numbers
- Design decisions or architectural patterns borrowed from external sources
- Quotes or paraphrased ideas from papers, docs, or books
- Tool versions, specifications, or protocol descriptions (e.g. MCP spec, Kubernetes API)

### What does NOT need a citation

- Your own implementation decisions and observations
- General knowledge in the field (e.g. "Python is an interpreted language")
- Results you measured yourself

### Adding entries to example.bib

Use **biblatex field names** (not plain BibTeX). Key fields per type:

```bibtex
@ONLINE{key,           % for websites, docs, specs
  author    = {Name},  % or publisher = {Org} if no author
  title     = {Title},
  date      = {2024},
  url       = {https://...},
  urldate   = {2026-03-10},
  langid    = {english}
}

@BOOK{key,
  author    = {Last, First},
  title     = {Title},
  date      = {2023},
  publisher = {Publisher},
  langid    = {english}
}

@ARTICLE{key,
  author       = {Last, First},
  title        = {Title},
  journaltitle = {Journal Name},
  date         = {2023},
  volume       = {1},
  number       = {1},
  pages        = {1--10},
  langid       = {english}
}
```

- Use `date` not `year` (biblatex style)
- Use `location` not `address`
- Use `journaltitle` not `journal`
- Separate multiple authors/editors with ` and `
- Do NOT put `%` comments inside `@ENTRY{}` blocks — bibtex does not treat `%` as a comment inside entries

## Footnotes and URLs

- Footnotes: `\footnote{text}`
- URLs: `\url{https://...}` (from the `url` package)

## Source Code Listings

- Block: `\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python]...\end{lstlisting}` (listings package)
- Inline: `\verb|code|` or `\texttt{code}`

## Index Entries

- Single entry: `\index{term}`
- Ranged: `\index{term|(}` ... `\index{term|)}`
- Sort key: `\index{alpha@$\alpha$}`
- Hierarchical: `\index{parent!child}`


